unch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-6b2f1c, max_parallel=1)\nspawn run started: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-6b2f1c)\nr6 rework against reviewer verdict 05 (CHANGES REQUESTED, F1 + F2). Both findings independently re-verified before acceptance and both CONFIRMED. No entitlement decision changed and no target row moved - both are cases of a statement about the contract contradicting the contract. F1: the reviewer jq gate was re-run against r5 (false/exit 1) and the finding was then widened - a sweep of every string in the r5 JSON found a SECOND stale claim the reviewer had not named (appGroupStyleRule per family, true of r4 two records, false since r5 deleted the probe record). So the class is closed rather than the string patched: new rule S1 (crossPlatformSharingContract) gives every cross-platform sentence a structured rule, R32 derives each grant set from the target rows, cross-checks it against the record own consumedByTargets, then requires the grantClause verbatim, the names inside it to equal the derived set, and every other target named in the sentence to be a declared non-grantee mention with its own clause; R33 requires the same clauses in the rationale document so the two artifacts cannot drift; and a count scan requires any App Group record-count claim to sit at a registered path and match the record list. The count scan failed on its first run on appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule - a THIRD stale claim (r4 two records) that neither the reviewer nor r5 had spotted - now fixed. One deliberate divergence from the literal rework text, stated in the JSON as whyTheMacOSHostIsNotNamed: the corrected sentence excludes macOS universally instead of naming the macOS host, because naming a target is exactly what went stale;