started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-26c930)\nReviewer verdict 06: changes requested. Authoritative matrix is r6, but Ceremony C1 task TASK-260728-q5kjta still authorizes rejected/superseded r5; independent freshness gate false, exit 1. Full evidence: TASK-260715-ypo7yo_reviewer-verdict-06.md. Route: analysis.\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-26c930, pid=43067, exit=0)\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override (preferred_agentic_system: mixed[claude,codex], config: spawn.preferred_agentic_system)\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-36bfba, max_parallel=1)\nspawn run started: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-36bfba)\nr7 rework against reviewer verdict 06 (CHANGES REQUESTED). The single blocking finding F1 was independently re-verified before being accepted: the reviewer's jq freshness gate re-run verbatim against r6 returned false/exit 1, and the C1 record's mutation set was then checked field by field against c1AuthorizationScope independently of the reviewer's claim that it matched - all four authorized App IDs present with a right boundary, Network Extensions and Mac Development wording present, ADR-024 cited, all four iOS App IDs and works.relux.proxy only inside a negation. CONFIRMED, and the revision label was the only defect, so the mutation set is preserved byte-for-byte. The class was then swept rather than assumed bounded: all 402 live board elements scanned for a matrix revision label, finding exactly two - q5kjta.scope (the finding) and this task's own notes field, which the reviewer did not report and which is NOT a defect because notes is append-only history; that distinction is why rule A1's boardGate declares excludedFields. All four required rework items done: q5kjta.scope re-pointed r5 -> r7 (character delta 0, mutation set untouched); the board gate extended from one live consumer to both, with the authorization-node check requiring the current revision and the exact c1AuthorizationScope; 13 board-record negative gates added, including the r6 defect verbatim; the stale check-portal-consumer.sh reference corrected in the rationale AND in the validator docstring, which carried the same stale name. The digest alternative the verdict offered is rejected with reasoning recorded in the contract. The class is closed by rule A1 plus a derived banned-revision set plus amendmentRule now requiring every amendment to re-point both pinned consumers, which R34 enforces - that omission was the actual root cause. The new negative gates found two defects in the gates themselves: the r6 right-boundary regex could not see a sentence-final identifier, and a 220-char negation window let an adjacent clause's NOT launder an authorization; both fixed, and negationWindow was removed rather than left vestigial. Gates: validate_matrix.py 1024 checks exit 0; mutate.py 130/130 exit 0; preserve.py 57 assertions exit 0; check-portal-consumer.py 43 checks over 2 consumers exit 0; reviewer jq gate true exit 0; check-legacy-preservation.sh exit 0; task-board validate exit 0; swift test 335 tests in 29 suites exit 0. All three harnesses verified to run in place from the board resource layout. One board field changed on one element: TASK-260728-q5kjta.scope. No board element created, deleted, re-parented or re-linked. No portal mutation, no product source, spec file or generated project touched.\nagent completed: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-36bfba, pid=45795, exit=0)\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override (preferred_agentic_system: mixed[claude,codex], config: spawn.preferred_agentic_system)\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-764d65, max_parallel=1)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-764d65)\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-764d65, pid=76240, exit=0)\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override (preferred_agentic_system: mixed[claude,codex], config: spawn.preferred_agentic_system)\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-75338f, max_parallel=1)\nspawn run started: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-75338f)\nr8 rework against reviewer verdict 07 (CHANGES REQUESTED, F1 + F2). Both findings were re-derived from the authoritative rows before being accepted and both CONFIRMED: F1 - ios.probe.host and ios.probe.provider carry application-groups status prohibited, so A5's 'present on every iOS bundle' and A18's 'ABSENT from both iOS probe bundles' cannot both hold; F2 - all four iOS profile rows declare development.devices as ['deferred - ADR-024'] while the four macOS rows declare this Mac, so A9's universal claim is false for half the rows. Both reviewer gates re-run against r7 returned false/exit 1. Fix: A5 rescoped to the two production iOS rows with the probe pair handed to A18 as a declared contrast; A9 split into A9a (four macOS Mac Development profiles), A9b (four iOS development profiles, deferred under ADR-024, not created at C1) and A9c (the two Developer ID profiles). Neither is left as a corrected string. r6 closed this exact class for the cross-platform SUMMARY (rule S1) and left the ASSERTION LIST - the other prose projection of the same rows - unchecked, which is why two assertions sat stale under a 1024-check validator. New rule S2 derives every scope-bearing assertion's scope three ways (class predicates over the rows, an explicit list, the row statuses), requires all three to agree, enforces a PARTITION per entitlement key (present union absent = all rows, present intersect absent = empty - exactly what A5 and A18 violated), and enforces coverage in both directions so a future assertion naming an entitlement key without registering its scope fails closed. All ten scope-bearing entitlement assertions and all three profile assertions are registered, not just the two reported. A THIRD stale claim was found here and reported by no verdict: the rationale document's own A6 table row still granted macos.host the Keychain group r5 removed under rule K2 - the verdict-05 F1 defect one revision later and one file over, invisible to R33 because R33 renders grantClauses, not assertions. The table also had no rows at all for A17 and A18. Recorded in S2.staleClaimsFound, fixed, and gated. Two defects in the NEW gates were found by their own negative mutations and fixed rather than worked around: R35's mention scan raised KeyError on a deleted assertion instead of failing closed, which silently suppressed four pre-existing [A-set] gates; and the first R27 banned phrase 'every iOS bundle' would have blocked A14's true absence claim, so it was narrowed to the defective presence shape. Gates: validate_matrix.py 36 rules 1331 checks exit 0; mutate.py 164/164 negative gates exit 0 (34 new for verdict 07, including both r7 texts replayed verbatim); check-portal-consumer.py 44 checks over 2 consumers exit 0; preserve.py 94 assertions exit 0, including a top-level check that the only keys differing from r7 are revision, supersedes, revisionLog and verification; both reviewer gates flipped to true/exit 0; check-legacy-preservation.sh exit 0; task-board validate exit 0; swift test 335 tests in 29 suites exit 0. All three harnesses re-verified running in place from the board resource layout; every command run without a pipeline because zsh carries no PIPESTATUS. One board field changed on one element: TASK-260728-q5kjta.scope, revision label r7 -> r8, character delta 0, verified byte-exact. No board element created, deleted, re-parented or re-linked; no portal mutation; no product source, spec file or generated project touched. Ready for review.\nagent completed: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-75338f, pid=79427, exit=0)\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override (preferred_agentic_system: mixed[claude,codex], config: spawn.preferred_agentic_system)\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-ae0e2a, max_parallel=1)\nspawn run started: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-ae0e2a)\nr9 self-audit after the r8 handoff. THERE IS NO VERDICT 08 - this revision was not requested by a reviewer; it reports what the audit found. r8 was re-run untouched first and was intact (1331 checks, 164/164 negative gates, 44 board checks, all exit 0), so nothing here repairs drift that had already appeared. Two defect classes found and closed. RULE D1: the matrix has carried a prose consumers list since r1 and it was never compared against the board dependency graph. Transitive closure over every task and bug record: 19 declared consumers, 16 reachable, 3 NOT - TASK-260715-1o9wjz, TASK-260715-3f4lxy and TASK-260715-29ws8l had no dependency path to this task at all, so a max_parallel=1 scheduler could have run the macOS credential resolver, the snapshot loader and the profile-trust contract while this matrix sat in analysis under its eighth changes-requested verdict. This is the verdict-05 F2 class one field over: verdict 05 checked what a consumer SAYS, rules A1/P1 check which revision it NAMES, and nothing checked WHEN it runs - A1/P1 only cover the two consumers carrying a revision label, and for every other consumer a missing edge fails silently because nothing reads a prose list. Four more elements carrying obligations were absent from consumers entirely, three of them assigned from inside PROSE fields (legacy.migrationDecisionOwner named TASK-260715-35nc5m for eight revisions; two sentences named TASK-260717-xempiv for five), which is why D1's derived set scans every string in the contract rather than three named lists. Fix: ONE board edge, 29ws8l blocked by this task, cycle-checked over the whole graph first - it orders all three because 29ws8l already blocks the other two, and it is semantically real, since 29ws8l AC2 specifies App Group and providerConfiguration data and AC4 specifies Keychain accessibility and access group, exactly what G4 and K2 decide. Gate R37 plus a new D1 block in check-portal-consumer.py reading blockedBy for tasks AND bugs make a missing edge fail closed; reachability is transitive; done is explicitly NOT an exemption basis because having finished is evidence a consumer RAN, which is the failure D1 catches; the one upstream exemption is verified upstream rather than accepted. RULE N1: the other half of the count class r6 left open - section 9.1 records it biting already, r3's prose claiming a number over a list holding a different one, r4 correcting it by hand, r5 moving it again when K2 withdrew the keychain group, while r6's scan covers App Group RECORD counts only. N1 derives allowlist counts and the rendered key list from the rows in both artifacts, pins its exclusion lists because widening an exclusion is the quiet way to neuter a scan, and states its bound rather than implying completeness. Three defects in the NEW gates were found by their own negative mutations and fixed rather than worked around: R37's reverse check was TAUTOLOGICAL because it counted the consumers list itself as a mention, so appending a consumer id present nowhere else passed; a graph mutation premised on cutting 3jloqy's edge orphaned nothing because q5kjta also blocks it and carries its own edge, so the premise was wrong rather than the gate, and it is also the argument for a closure over an edge check; and a document mutation did not apply against a hard-wrapped sentence. The fixed reverse check immediately found TASK-260715-33oofa - a declared consumer the contract never explained, its obligation stated inside the macOS task's entry while 33oofa is the iOS half carrying the opposite instruction - now recorded. Also recorded, not smoothed over: the board resource store does not preserve the executable bit, so a re-registered .sh exits 126 on a ./ run and must be invoked via zsh. Gates: validate_matrix.py 37 rules 2483 checks exit 0; mutate.py 202/202 negative gates exit 0 (38 new - 20 D1, 18 N1 - including a new dependency-GRAPH harness, because D1's reachability half cannot be tested by corrupting the contract, which is why eight revisions of a 1331-check validator never saw it); check-portal-consumer.py 84 checks over 3 rules exit 0; preserve.py 122 assertions exit 0, including a top-level check that the only keys ADDED are consumerDependencyContract and numericClaimContract and the only keys CHANGED are revision, supersedes, revisionLog, consumers and downstreamConsequences; an independent gate that imports no project harness flips r8 false/exit 1 to r9 true/exit 0; check-legacy-preservation.sh exit 0; task-board validate exit 0; swift test 335 tests in 29 suites exit 0. All four matrix harnesses re-verified running in place from the board resource layout; every command run without a pipeline because zsh carries no PIPESTATUS. No entitlement decision, target row, profile row, record, assertion, 