# TASK-260715-ypo7yo — handoff evidence, revision `2026-07-28.r9`

**There is no reviewer verdict 08.** r8 was handed off to review; this revision is a
**self-audit** of the artifact in that interval, and it reports what the audit found rather
than what a reviewer asked for. r8 was re-run untouched first and was **intact** — 1331
checks, 164/164 negative gates, 44 board checks, all exit 0 — so nothing here is credited to
repairing drift that had already appeared.

**No entitlement decision, target row, profile row, record, assertion, assertion scope or
C1 mutation set changed.** 122 preservation assertions prove it, including a top-level check
that the only keys added are `consumerDependencyContract` and `numericClaimContract` and the
only keys changed are `revision`, `supersedes`, `revisionLog`, `consumers` and
`downstreamConsequences`.

## Rule D1 — three declared consumers could run before this contract was accepted

The contract has carried a prose `consumers` list since r1. **It was never compared against
the board's dependency graph.** Reachability was computed as a transitive closure over every
task and bug record: **19 declared consumers, 16 reachable, 3 not.**

| consumer | what it consumes | state at r8 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TASK-260715-1o9wjz` | the macOS resolver reads the system-domain keychain item, never an access group (K1, K2) | no dependency path to this task |
| `TASK-260715-3f4lxy` | the snapshot loader cannot read a macOS App Group container (G4) | no dependency path to this task |
| `TASK-260715-29ws8l` | its **AC2** specifies what travels in "App Group and `providerConfiguration` data"; its **AC4** specifies "Keychain accessibility, access group" — exactly what G4 and K2 decide | no path, **and it blocks the other two** |

With `max_parallel=1` the scheduler was free to run all three while this matrix sat in
`analysis` under its eighth changes-requested verdict. Each would have been authored against
an unaccepted matrix, and 29ws8l without this matrix reproduces precisely the iOS-only
assumption **OC-1** was raised against.

Rules **A1** and **P1** exist because a consumer that names a *revision* can name a stale
one. Every other consumer names no revision, so the dependency edge is the only thing
ordering it after acceptance — and a missing edge fails **silently**, because nothing reads a
prose list. This is the **verdict-05 F2 class one field over**: verdict 05 checked what a
consumer *says*, A1/P1 check which revision it names, nothing checked *when it runs*.

**Four more elements** carrying obligations were absent from `consumers` altogether, three of
them assigned from inside prose fields — `legacy.migrationDecisionOwner` had named
`TASK-260715-35nc5m` for eight revisions and two sentences had named `TASK-260717-xempiv` for
five. That is why D1's derived set scans **every string** in the contract rather than three
named lists.

**Fix: one board edge.** `TASK-260715-29ws8l` blocked by this task orders all three, since it
already blocks the other two. The edge is semantically real, not a scheduling trick — see its
AC2/AC4 above — and the whole graph was cycle-checked before it was written. Gate **R37**
plus a new **D1 block** in `check-portal-consumer.py` make a missing edge fail closed;
`done` is explicitly **not** an exemption basis, and the one `upstream` exemption
(`TASK-260728-7ii1xz`) is **verified** upstream rather than taken on its word.

## Rule N1 — the other half of the count class r6 left open

§9.1 records this class biting once already: r3's prose claimed a number over a list holding
a different one, r4 corrected it **by hand**, and r5 moved it again when rule K2 withdrew the
keychain group. Three revisions, three numbers, three hand corrections. r6 built a count scan
after verdict 05 exposed a stale one — but only for App Group **record** counts, so the
allowlist-size class stayed unchecked in **both** artifacts for three more revisions.

Both surviving numbers were **recomputed** and are correct. Nothing was checking them. N1
registers five rendered claims across the JSON and the document, derives every number from
the rows, and for §9.1 also compares the **rendered key list** against the derived allowlist
in both directions — the check that would have caught the r3 defect at the time. The scan's
exclusion lists are **pinned**, because widening an exclusion is the quiet way to neuter a
scan, and its **bound is stated**: it finds `<number> keys`; two registered claims render
their number without that word, so registration found them, not the scan.

## Three defects in the new gates, found by their own negative mutations

Fixed rather than worked around, and all three recorded in the transcript:

1. **R37's reverse check was tautological.** It compared the declared set against ids
   mentioned *anywhere* — and the consumers list is itself a string in the contract, so the
   question answered itself. A mutation appending a consumer id present nowhere else
   **passed**. Now read from everywhere *else*, in both the validator and the board gate.
2. **A graph mutation's premise was wrong, not the gate.** Cutting `TASK-260715-3jloqy`'s
   edge orphans nothing, because `TASK-260728-q5kjta` also blocks it and carries its own
   edge. Replaced by two accurate mutations. This is also the argument for a transitive
   closure over an edge check: the DAG has redundant paths.
3. A document mutation did not apply against a hard-wrapped sentence; it now spans the real
   line break.

## One finding the new check produced, fixed here

R37's reverse direction immediately caught `TASK-260715-33oofa`: **declared a consumer since
r1 and never explained anywhere.** Its obligation was stated inside `TASK-260715-uyju7n`'s
entry — the *macOS* task — while 33oofa is the iOS half carrying the opposite instruction
(both iOS production targets **must** declare the App Group and the keychain group; G1's
Register App Groups setting applies *there and only there*; both iOS probes declare neither).
Now recorded in `downstreamConsequences` and in §10.1.

