        "com.apple.security.application-groups": {
          "status": "prohibited",
          "portalCapability": false,
          "rationale": "Withheld under least privilege (rule G4): no grant this entitlement confers on macOS is used by the selected design. The six grants were read out of the system App Sandbox profile rather than assumed - three are home-relative and break across the root/user boundary, three are system-wide and belong to mechanisms this project does not use (Mac App Store group content, a group-prefixed XPC Mach service, group-prefixed POSIX IPC). The host-to-provider channel selected by TASK-260728-7ii1xz is providerConfiguration plus a sendProviderMessage seed into a provider-owned system-domain keychain item, and its XPC candidate - the only one that would have needed a group-prefixed Mach name - was rejected. A private group container is redundant with the container every sandboxed target already gets unconditionally (application.sb:107-108).",
          "evidence": "/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71): the entitlement appears at lines 302-330 and 459-465 and nowhere else. Full per-grant survey in appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.survey.",
          "reopensOnly": "This row reopens if, and only if, one of three things becomes true, each of which is a grant in the survey above rather than a general appeal to App Groups: (a) the macOS host-to-provider channel becomes a group-prefixed Mach service, i.e. the XPC candidate is revived; (b) it becomes a group-prefixed POSIX IPC object; or (c) the provider is moved out of root into a user context, which would make the group container genuinely shared. It does NOT reopen because a spec sentence mentions App Groups, because a shipping third-party product carries the key, or to spare a portal sitting. Reopening is an amendment under section 11, naming which of (a), (b) or (c) became true.",
          "decidedBy": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo r4, on TASK-260728-7ii1xz"
        },
        "keychain-access-groups": {
          "status": "prohibited",
          "portalCapability": false,
          "rationale": "Withheld under least privilege (rule K2). keychain-access-groups is a SHARING construct, and on macOS this contract has no second member to share with. The host's own Data Protection Keychain vault (TASK-260715-379cpk) does not need it: the com.apple.application-identifier entitlement that the signing toolchain injects into every provisioned macOS target is itself an access group, items stored in it are private to the app, and it becomes the default access group when no keychain-access-groups entitlement is present. The macOS provider cannot be the second member - it runs as root against the file-based system-domain keychain, which has no access groups at all (rule K1) - and neither probe target touches the Data Protection Keychain. The r4 rationale rested on a host-owned pre-seed staging item held across system-extension approval; that item is written and read by the host alone, so the default group serves it. Persistence across an interrupted approval flow is a property of the keychain, not of a shared access group.",
          "evidence": "Apple, Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/sharing-access-to-keychain-items-among-a-collection-of-apps): 'Xcode automatically adds the application-identifier entitlement (or the com.apple.application-identifier entitlement in macOS) to every app during code signing, formed as the team identifier (team ID) plus the bundle identifier (bundle ID).' / 'The system recognizes this app ID as the name of your app's default keychain access group by including it in your access group array ... Any keychain items stored with this access group are private to App One.' / 'If you don't specify any keychain access groups, then the app ID is the default.' Apple TN3137, On Mac keychain APIs and implementations (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/technotes/tn3137-on-mac-keychains): 'macOS builds the list of data protection keychain access groups available to your program from its code signing entitlements. These entitlements must be authorized by a provisioning profile.' macos.host is provisioned on both channels (rule R14), so its injected com.apple.application-identifier is profile-authorized and the default group is available on both. Both documents fetched 2026-07-28 from developer.apple.com.",
          "defaultAccessGroup": "262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac",
          "reopensOnly": "This row reopens if, and only if, a SECOND macOS target in this contract must read or write the same Data Protection Keychain item as the host. Today that would mean the provider moving out of root into a user context, because rule K1 excludes a root/system-domain provider from membership on technical grounds rather than policy ones. It does NOT reopen because the host wants a stable item name, because the iOS rows carry the group, because a shipping third-party macOS product declares the key (rule K1's shippingProductsAreNotEvidence clause applies here too), or to keep one cross-platform code path. Reopening is an amendment under section 11 that names the second member.",
          "decidedBy": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo r5, on reviewer verdict 04 F1"
