.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1913:        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:329",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1921:        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:459-465",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1928:    "privateContainerFinding": "TASK-260728-7ii1xz amendment M6 left the rows granted on the grounds that 'a private root-context container may still have uses this task did not survey'. Surveyed here, and the answer is no. (appsandbox-container-common) and (appsandbox-container-macos) at application.sb:107-108 are UNCONDITIONAL - they are not inside any (when (entitlement ...)) form - so a sandboxed target already has its own private container, derived from the application_container_id sandbox parameter, without any App Group entitlement. The only thing a group container adds over the container the provider already has is SHARING, and sharing is precisely what the root/user split breaks. A private group container is therefore strictly redundant, not merely unused.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1933:    "source": "/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71), read in full at lines 302-330 and 459-465. TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md sections 4, 5 and 6. Apple, Configuring app groups: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/configuring-app-groups",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:2035:      "why": "One edge, three gaps. TASK-260715-29ws8l already blocks TASK-260715-1o9wjz and TASK-260715-3f4lxy, so ordering it after this contract orders all three. The edge is semantically real, not a scheduling trick: 29ws8l AC2 requires it to specify what travels in 'App Group and providerConfiguration data' and AC4 requires it to specify 'Keychain accessibility, access group' — on macOS rule G4 prohibits the App Group on every target and rule K2 prohibits the keychain access group on every target, so authoring 29ws8l without this matrix reproduces exactly the iOS-only assumption OC-1 was raised against.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:2041:      "REACHABILITY — TASK-260715-3f4lxy: declared in `consumers`, assigned a downstream consequence, and named in OC-4.affects. Its three blockers were the credential-transport decision, TASK-260715-29ws8l, and one unrelated task, so it had no path here.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:2042:      "REACHABILITY — TASK-260715-29ws8l: named in OC-1.affects. Its two blockers were the credential-transport decision and one unrelated task, so it had no path here — and it blocks the other two, which is why one edge closes all three.",
